@* Principle 10 – Text is readable and understandable *@

<h2>Identify the page language</h2>
<p>
    The <code>lang</code> attribute on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> tells screen readers which voice and pronunciation
    rules to use, helps browsers offer translation, and helps with hyphenation and spell-checking. Without it,
    a screen reader set to French may read English text with a French accent. Every page on this site starts with:
</p>
<code-sample kind="good" label="Page language">
    <html lang="en">
        …
    </html>
</code-sample>

<h2>Identify changes in language</h2>
<p>
    When a passage is in a different language, mark it with its own <code>lang</code> attribute. Listen to this
    section with a screen reader; the voice changes for the quotation.
</p>
<blockquote lang="es">
    <p>“El poder de la Web está en su universalidad. El acceso por cualquier persona, independientemente de la discapacidad, es un aspecto esencial.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>
    Translation: “The power of the Web is in its universality. Access by everyone regardless of disability is an essential aspect.”
    (Tim Berners-Lee, inventor of the World Wide Web)
</p>
<p>
    It works for short phrases too: good design has a certain <span lang="fr">je ne sais quoi</span>.
</p>
<code-sample label="Marking a change of language">
    <blockquote lang="es">
        <p>El poder de la Web está en su universalidad…</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Good design has a certain <span lang="fr">je ne sais quoi</span>.</p>
</code-sample>

<h2>Define unusual words and abbreviations</h2>
<p>
    Expand abbreviations the first time they appear, and define jargon. HTML has elements for this:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <abbr title="Accessible Rich Internet Applications">ARIA</abbr> attributes add meaning to custom widgets.
        (<code>&lt;abbr title="…"&gt;</code>, which shows a tooltip on hover. Because tooltips aren't available to keyboard or
        touch users, it is still best to write the expansion in the text.)
    </li>
    <li>
        A <dfn id="def-screen-reader">screen reader</dfn> is software that reads the content of the screen aloud or
        outputs it to a braille display. (<code>&lt;dfn&gt;</code> marks the term being defined.)
    </li>
</ul>
<p>A glossary is another good option:</p>
<dl>
    <dt>WCAG (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines)</dt>
    <dd>The international standard for web accessibility, published by the W3C.</dd>
    <dt>Assistive technology</dt>
    <dd>Hardware or software that helps people with disabilities use a computer, such as screen readers, magnifiers, and switch devices.</dd>
    <dt>“Piece of cake” (idiom)</dt>
    <dd>Very easy. Idioms like this can confuse non-native speakers and people who interpret language literally, so avoid them or explain them.</dd>
</dl>

<h2>Write in plain language</h2>
<p>
    Use short sentences, common words, and the active voice. Put the most important information first.
    Plain language helps everyone, including people with cognitive disabilities, non-native speakers, and busy readers.
</p>
<div class="compare">
    <div class="example example-bad">
        <h3>Don't: bury the meaning</h3>
        <p>
            Pursuant to departmental policy, students who have not effectuated the requisite registration procedures prior
            to the aforementioned deadline shall be precluded from participation in laboratory sessions for the duration
            of the term.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="example example-good">
        <h3>Do: say it plainly</h3>
        <p>
            Register by Friday. If you don't, you can't attend labs this semester.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
<p>
    When complex text can't be avoided (for example legal terms), provide a simplified summary next to it.
</p>
<details class="card">
    <summary>Read a simplified summary of the course's late-work policy</summary>
    <ul>
        <li>You can turn in work up to 2 days late.</li>
        <li>You lose 10% of the grade for each late day.</li>
        <li>After 2 days, ask your instructor before submitting.</li>
    </ul>
</details>
